% ============================================================================
% elector-allocation-method — how a jurisdiction assigns its presidential
% electors (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "Do all states award their electoral votes the same way?" is the question
% behind one of the most-cited exceptions in U.S. civics, and the answer is
% no. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? elector_allocation_method(maine, $M)
%       % proportional
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `elector_allocation_method(jurisdiction, method)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query —
% the engine returns the method WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any jurisdiction the source does not place.
% The query also runs BACKWARD, which is how a learner is usually quizzed
% ("which states are the exception?") and returns BOTH named states:
%
%     ? elector_allocation_method($J, proportional)
%       % maine
%       % nebraska
%
% THIS FILE CLOSES A DOCUMENTED ABSTENTION. `electoral-college-count.adj`
% holds `winner_take_all_states -> 48` and deliberately declined the
% Maine/Nebraska fact, its header recording that a proportional system is
% "a METHOD, not a count ... Different axis, its own future table". This is
% that table. The two compose rather than overlap: the count table says HOW
% MANY jurisdictions are winner-take-all, this one says WHICH METHOD a
% named jurisdiction uses — and the 48 is exactly the set this table's
% first row names, which is why the group row is kept rather than tabling
% only the two exceptions. Shipping the exception without the rule it is an
% exception TO would leave a learner able to recall that Maine is
% proportional without being able to recall that almost nowhere else is.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "Electoral College" page, two
% consecutive sentences:
%
%     jurisdiction (this table's atom)   method (this table's atom)   quote (verbatim)
%     ---------------------------------  ---------------------------  -----------------------------------------------
%     forty_eight_states_and_dc          winner_take_all              "In 48 states and Washington, D.C., the winner
%                                                                        gets all the electoral votes for that state."
%     maine                              proportional                 "Maine and Nebraska assign their electors using
%     nebraska                           proportional                    a proportional system." (one sentence names
%                                                                        both states)
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch). `trust authoritative` —
% USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's official guide to
% government information, a first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact
% being recalled, the same tier and the SAME page
% `electoral-college-count.adj` already cites.
%
% ON NAMING THE GROUP ROW. The first atom is `forty_eight_states_and_dc`
% because that is how the source itself delimits the group — "In 48 states
% and Washington, D.C.". A vaguer atom like `most_states` would have been a
% paraphrase, since the page never says "most"; a bare `other_states` would
% have hidden the fact that the source states a specific count. The count
% is the source's own and it also travels, with its citation, in
% `electoral-college-count.adj`'s `winner_take_all_states` row, so if a
% future apportionment changes the number, both rows are re-derived from
% the same re-fetched sentence rather than one silently disagreeing with
% the other.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - The page states that the winner "gets all the electoral votes for
%     that state" and that Maine and Nebraska use "a proportional system",
%     but it does NOT explain HOW the proportional system apportions them
%     (by congressional district, statewide, or otherwise). A recall for
%     the mechanism abstains rather than filling in the well-known details
%     from outside the source.
%   - Individual states other than Maine and Nebraska are not rows. The
%     source describes the other 48 only as a group, so binding a specific
%     state name would require deciding it is one of the 48 — an inference
%     the page does not license for any particular state.
%   - The faithless-elector penalties the same page lists (fined,
%     disqualified, replaced by a substitute elector, prosecuted by their
%     state) are consequences for an INDIVIDUAL elector's vote, not a
%     jurisdiction's allocation method, and the list states no second axis
%     uniformly — only one of the four names who imposes it — so it is not
%     tabled here or anywhere yet.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table elector_allocation_method {
    columns jurisdiction, method

    row (forty_eight_states_and_dc, winner_take_all)
    row (maine, proportional)
    row (nebraska, proportional)

    source "In 48 states and Washington, D.C., the winner gets all the electoral votes for that state."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/electoral-college"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Maine and Nebraska assign their electors using a proportional system." locator "https://www.usa.gov/electoral-college"
}
